{"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-140","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"140","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Bureau of Land Management relating to Public Land Order No. 7917 for Withdrawal of Federal Lands; Cook, Lake, and Saint Louis Counties, MN.","introducedDate":"2026-01-12","latestActionDate":"2026-04-27","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-85.","policyArea":"Public Lands and Natural Resources","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Alternative and renewable resources","Department of the Interior","Lakes and rivers","Land use and conservation","Marine pollution","Mining","Minnesota"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001212","cosponsorBioguideIds":["F000475","M001228","B001323","L000596"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution opens approximately 225,504 acres of National Forest System lands in Cook, Lake, and Saint Louis Counties in Minnesota to mineral and geothermal leasing. Specifically, the joint resolution nullifies Public Land Order 7917 , which withdrew such land from mineral and geothermal leasing for 20 years beginning in 2023. The Bureau of Land Management issued the order to protect and preserve the Rainy River Watershed, the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness, the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness Mining Protection Area, and the 1854 Ceded Territory of the Lake Superior Chippewa from potential adverse effects of mineral and geothermal exploration and development. The joint resolution removes such protections and allows such land to be leased for the exploration and development of minerals and geothermal energy.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/140","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hjres/BILLSTATUS-119hjres140.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:16.333Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hjres-140","plainSummary":"This resolution would reverse a federal order that withdrew approximately 225,504 acres of national forest land in northeastern Minnesota from mineral and geothermal leasing. The original order, issued in 2023, was intended to protect the Rainy River Watershed and the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness from potential mining impacts for 20 years. If passed, the resolution would reopen these lands to mineral exploration and development, as well as geothermal energy leasing.","industries":[{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill would directly reopen over 225,000 acres to mineral exploration and leasing that are currently withdrawn from such activity."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The bill affects protections for wilderness areas and watersheds, directly implicating environmental and conservation advocacy organizations."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The bill would reopen the lands to geothermal energy leasing and development alongside mineral leasing."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"97c202ef42a9b3df03b4331a","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:33:12.785Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would open approximately 225,504 acres of National Forest System lands in Cook, Lake, and Saint Louis Counties in Minnesota to mineral and geothermal leasing.","whoItTouches":"People who live near or work on federal land. People who live near a mine, and people who work in one.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what may be done with that land. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hjres-140","bioguideId":"S001212","cycle":